This article provides a comprehensive list of acupuncture points, locations on the body used in [[acupuncture]], [[acupressure]], and other treatment systems based on [[Traditional Chinese Medicine|Traditional Chinese Medicine (TCM)]].

== Locations and basis ==

{{main|Meridian (Chinese medicine)}}

More than four hundred acupuncture points have been described, with the majority located on one of the twenty main cutaneous and subcutaneous [[meridian (Chinese medicine)|meridians]], pathways which run throughout the body and according to [[Traditional Chinese Medicine|Traditional Chinese Medicine (TCM)]] transport ''[[qi]]''. Twelve of these major meridians, commonly referred to as "the primary meridians", are bilateral and practitioners{{Who|date=December 2022}} associate them with internal organs. The remaining eight meridians are designated{{By whom|date=December 2022}} as "extraordinary", and are also bilateral except for three, one that encircles the body near the waist, and two that run along the midline of the body. Only those two extraordinary meridians that run along the midline contain their own points, the remaining six comprise points from the aforementioned twelve primary meridians. There are also points that are not located on the fourteen major meridians but do lie in the complete nexus referred to as ''Jīngluò'' ({{lang|zh|經絡}}). Such outliers are often{{Specify|date=December 2022}} referred to as "extra points".<ref name=Deadman2007>Deadman, P, Baker K, Al-Khafaji, M. 2007. A Manual of Acupuncture, 2nd Edition. Journal of Chinese Medicine Publications. {{ISBN|978-0951054659}}.</ref>

There is no anatomical and physiological basis for acupuncture points and meridians.<ref name=Napadow2008>Napadow V, Ahn A, Longhurst J, et al. 2008. [https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3155097/ The status and future of acupuncture mechanism research.]  J Altern Complement Med 14(7): 861–869.</ref> In practice, acupuncture points are located{{By whom|date=December 2022}} by a combination of anatomical landmarks, [[palpation]], and feedback from the patient.<ref name=Deadman2007/>

